// Vendor:      systemd journald (Linux)
// Wire:        one line of `journalctl -o json` (UTF-8 JSON object)
// Upstream:    ingress (raw wire)
//              Transport-layer parser, run directly off `ingress`
//              (typically the first process in a pipeline whose `input`
//              is `type journal`).
// Output:      workspace.journald.* — all fields as journald exposes them.
//              This is a thin LOTL wrapper: limpid's `input journal`
//              emits ingress as `journalctl -o json`-byte-identical JSON,
//              and this snippet just hands it to `parse_json`. No
//              vendor-specific decoding happens here — field names stay
//              journald-canonical so downstream composers (e.g.
//              `compose_rfc5424`) can read them by their original name.
//              Field naming:
//                PRIORITY               — syslog severity (0-7), JSON string ("6")
//                SYSLOG_FACILITY        — syslog facility (0-23), JSON string ("4")
//                SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER      — program name (e.g. "sshd")
//                _COMM                  — actual binary name (fallback for IDENTIFIER)
//                _PID / SYSLOG_PID      — process id, JSON string
//                _HOSTNAME              — host where the entry was recorded
//                _SYSTEMD_UNIT          — owning systemd unit (e.g. "ssh.service")
//                __REALTIME_TIMESTAMP   — wall-clock microseconds since epoch, string
//                __MONOTONIC_TIMESTAMP  — boot-relative microseconds, string
//                MESSAGE                — log line text
//              Numeric-looking fields stay JSON strings (journalctl
//              convention); convert with `to_int(...)` if you need
//              arithmetic (e.g. PRI). Binary-valued fields (rare; e.g.
//              COREDUMP) surface as arrays of integers, not strings —
//              treat with care if your downstream is text-only.
// Test corpus: spec-only (journalctl JSON export format; the parser is
//              a passthrough `parse_json` wrapper so no per-shape
//              verification applies — downstream parsers like
//              parse_openssh carry their own corpus).
//
// Sample wire:
//   {"__CURSOR":"s=...","__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP":"1714400000000000",
//    "PRIORITY":"6","SYSLOG_FACILITY":"4","SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER":"sshd",
//    "_PID":"12345","_HOSTNAME":"edge01","_SYSTEMD_UNIT":"ssh.service",
//    "MESSAGE":"Accepted publickey for alice from 192.0.2.10 port 51234 ssh2"}
//
// Typical pipeline (illustrative):
//   process parse_journald | compose_rfc5424
//   process parse_journald | parse_openssh | compose_ocsf

def process parse_journald {
    workspace.journald = parse_json(ingress)
}
